  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of community NGOs, Women Groups and CSOs partnerships established raising awareness</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">This indicator measures the extent to which partnerships have been formed between community-based NGOs, women&#x2019;s groups, and civil society organizations (CSOs) with the purpose of raising public awareness on specific issues&#x2014;such as governance, human rights, security sector reform, or women&#x2019;s political participation.

It tracks the quantity of such partnerships established during a reporting period, reflecting progress in building collaborative networks that can amplify advocacy messages, extend outreach, and mobilize communities for collective action. A higher number indicates increased coordination, resource sharing, and joint efforts to influence public attitudes and behaviors.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="0" year="2020"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="8"></target>
     <actual value="8"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of mobile legal aid desk/clinics established within and outside the greater Banjul area</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">This indicator measures the total number of mobile legal aid desks or clinics that have been set up both within the Greater Banjul Area and in other regions of The Gambia to provide accessible legal services to communities.

The focus is on expanding legal aid coverage&#x2014;especially to underserved and rural populations&#x2014;by creating mobile, on-the-ground service points where individuals can seek legal advice, information, and representation.

A higher number of such mobile units indicates improved geographic reach, reduced barriers to accessing justice, and greater capacity to respond to legal needs of vulnerable groups, including women, youth, and marginalized communities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="3" year="2020"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="6"></target>
     <actual value="8"></actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="6"></target>
     <actual value="8"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of cases addressed through the established legal aid desks/clinics disaggregated by age and sex)</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">This indicator tracks the total number of legal cases handled through the legal aid desks or clinics that have been established, with the data broken down by the age and sex of the clients served.

It measures the actual utilization and effectiveness of these legal aid services in responding to community needs&#x2014;covering issues such as criminal defense, civil disputes, family matters, or rights violations.

Disaggregating by age and sex helps assess who is benefiting most from the services, identify gaps in access among specific demographic groups, and ensure that legal aid provision is inclusive, equitable, and responsive to the needs of both women and men across different age groups.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="36" year="2020"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
